<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali Mohammed Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1748">1748</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali Mohammed Khan was the founder of Rohilla power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a>, the region east of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. Adopted son of the Afghan chief Daud Khan, he built a following of Rohilla soldiers from the 1720s, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aonla">Aonla</a> his capital and won recognition from the Mughal emperor. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizullah-khan">Faizullah Khan</a> later founded the princely state of Rampur.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ali Muhammad Khan was born around 1707 into a Jat family and was originally named Prem Singh Nain. As a child he was captured by Daud Khan Barech, the Barech chief, during a conflict with rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>; impressed by the boy, Daud adopted him, converted him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and gave him a new name. According to the Inradus Sa'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a>, he was a Hanafi Muslim of the Qadiri Sufi order.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He succeeded his foster father as chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> at the age of fourteen and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> into a powerful state. He distinguished himself by helping to suppress a rebellion of the Barha family, whose chief had put the Mughal governor Marhamat Khan and all his followers to death, and as a reward the emperor Muhammad Shah gave him the title of Nawab in 1737. He was generally regarded as a ruler who did not oppress the people.</p><p>In 1746 a quarrel over the cutting of wood between the construction workers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safdar-jang">Safdar Jang</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> and Ali Muhammad's forest guards led Safdar Jang to seek his ruin. Through Qamar-ud-Din Khan he told Muhammad Shah that the Rohilla chief meant to found his own sultanate. The emperor sent an expedition against him and he was imprisoned, but he was later pardoned and made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirhind">Sirhind</a>. He afterwards returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> and ruled it as an independent state until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ali Muhammad Khan died on 15 September 1748. Hafiz Rahmat Khan Barech had sworn on the Qur'an to carry out his dying wishes, but took power as regent instead. Of his sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizullah-khan">Faizullah Khan</a> later founded the state of Rampur. In the 19th century the Nawabs of Rampur claimed that Ali Muhammad had been a Barha Sayyid and took the title of Sayyid, and they hired a prominent maulvi of Rampur, Najmul Ghani, to trace their ancestry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>. They could present no pedigree or proof, and the claim was generally rejected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali Mohammed Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the British-backed Nawab of Awadh defeated the Rohillas in 1774, the Rohilla chief Faizullah Khan, son of <b>Ali Muhammad Khan</b>, was allowed to keep Rampur and surrounding lands by the Treaty of Lal Dhang.</blockquote><a href="/state/rampur-state">Rampur State</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ali Muhammad Khan</b>, adopted son of a Rohilla chief, built a power base there from the 1720s, making Aonla his capital, and was recognised by the Mughal court as nawab.</blockquote><a href="/state/rohilla-state">Rohilla State</a></li></ul></section>
